  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStrong ridge of high pressure south of the region keeps zonal flow \nover the area today with westerly flow. A shortwave tough is\nmoving through this morning kicking off a line of showers over\ncentral NY. Once this line moves through there could be another\npush of morning showers. A weak cold front is expected to move \nthrough the region later this afternoon and into this evening \nwith potential to kick off a few more showers. Overall CAMs \nshow isolated showers possible throughout the day, but \nconfidence on where exactly these develop is uncertain. Overall \nmost of today should lean dry with highs warming into the mid \n70s to low 80s with overnight temperatures in the low to mid \n60s.\n\nA more organized low is expected to track over the region on \nThursday bringing the potential for showers and thunderstorms. \nInitially a warm front will move through in the morning with a \ncold front following after. If the timing of the frontal passage\ntrends later, there could be a low severe thunderstorm risk \nwith greater than 30 knots of shear in place. Precipitable water\nvalues range 1.5-1.75 inches, with a warm could depth of 11-12k\nfeet, so any thunderstorms or showers that do form along the \nfront will have heavy rain. Highs again will warm into the mid \n70s to low 80s with overnight temperatures falling into the mid \n50s to low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure builds back into the area by Friday allowing \ndry conditions to persist through Saturday morning. Northwest flow \nalso settles in with cooler temperatures, highs will range in the \nupper 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nAnother system approaches the region this weekend as the ridge\nbegins to recede and a low pressure system advances from the\nMidwest. Much of Saturday should remain dry, though an\napproaching warm front may bring increasing chances for showers\nSaturday night into Sunday. Strengthening southwesterly flow\nahead of the low will transport abundant moisture into the\nregion through Sunday. The pattern then becomes increasingly\nunsettled as the frontal boundary stalls near the northern\nperiphery of the southeast ridge, with a series of waves\ntracking along it into early next week. The exact placement of\nthis boundary remains uncertain, which will ultimately \ndetermine where the heavies precipitation occurs. However model \nguidance continues to suggest the potential for several rounds \nof rainfall. Temperatures will remain cool through the weekend \ninto early next week, with highs generally in the low to mid 60s\nand lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s.",
